video games have tried a lot of silly  things to enhance shooters over the  years some like nintendo's humble rumble  pack and the haptic feedback and  controllers they would inspire have  stood the test of time and others like  this  not so much let's jump into some of the  silliest ideas video games have tried  that just didn't quite work out in their  own time hit that subscribe button give  us the old thumbs up let's get to it  if you were around in the late 1900s  specifically 1992 you probably watched  lawnmower man or read the book snow  crash and you were sold that all your  video games and possibly your entire  life would become part of virtual  reality very soon this was 100 the way  of the future and to sell that point  home malls all over the country set up  these kiosks where you could put on this  massive headset and play dactyl  nightmare it was a very simple pvp arena  shooter where you walked around lobbing  projectiles at other players teleporting  around the map and trying not to get  yeaded off a map by a dinosaur it's not  that vr was a bad idea in the 90s it's  just that we all thought these things  would be in our lives at that time and  that was laughable because the tech  wasn't nearly small enough or affordable  enough for that to happen for something  like 30 more years the cost was way too  high and computers weren't nearly strong  enough to bring that technology home  seriously at that time computers looked  like this  it just wasn't going to work then and to  be honest vr's still in pretty early  stages now  we all used to have crt televisions that  were ridiculously heavy and tough to  move around and apparently had a high  voltage capacitor inside that could kill  you if you took it apart even when it  was unplugged but it was totally worth  all the hassle to play light gun games  there were some great ones like duck  hunt a launch game for nes in 1985 and  my absolute favorite time crisis  then one day i remember walking into a  best buy and seeing my first flat screen  tv it looked like some kind of space  magic and cost about as much as space  magic would at something like 10 000  i had no idea what i was really looking  at was the end of light gun games at  home as people bought these tvs  accessories like the nes zapper and gun  cons for ps2 simply stopped working so  it turns out crt televisions allow for  more precise measurement of where a  light gun was pointing at the screen the  details are a bit complicated so i'll  link some videos below light gun games  didn't instantly give up and disappear  it was more of a slow death and it  wasn't for developers lack of trying to  keep them alive consoles like the wii  xbox 360 and playstation 3 all tried to  carry on using a sensor bar that was  placed on top or just below a television  but the way it behaved was more like  dragging a cursor around your television  screen it was never very precise or  satisfying the likes that consoles  continued after that to experiment in  order to track your aim led to some even  funnier inventions like the ps3's rave  stick looking playstation move  controller that you could use with  killzone 3. now according to one linus  tech tips that technology may have just  finally caught up to flat screen  televisions so light gun games could  make a return  [Music]  oh boy the kinect for xbox 360 and xbox  one i need to start off by saying i'm  probably one of the few people who loves  present tense connect and i still use it  to this day for exercise games it was  there for me with hand gestures and  voice commands when i was elbow deep in  my kids diapers and couldn't exactly  touch things  and a huge source of relief early in the  pandemic when we couldn't leave the  house but still needed outlets for  movement  connect tried a lot of things and i love  that developers were really grappling  with the technology it was great for all  kinds of things like dancing  but boy did it not work out for shooters  for technical and also kind of creepy  reasons on the technical side when it  comes to shooters the kinect just  doesn't work it was one thing in the  past to be holding a fake toy gun but  now you were standing there with nothing  in your hands just completely imagining  a weapon or something sometimes you even  had to pretend you were a weapon it was  just really goofy for all parties  involved the most memorable attempt was  ghost recon future soldier using connect  to aim and shoot weapons as well as  navigate their gunsmith you had to take  this kind of awkward horse stance aim  with a fist and then quickly open and  close your hand on the screen to fire it  to be fair this did lead to a pretty  great trailer with coco ice-t's special  someone who awkwardly showed off this  mechanic and generally looked like she  could kill a man so i'll probably stop  talking now there were some other  creative attempts that sort of worked  like gunstringer where you control the  marionette and use your fingers to shoot  my kids had a pretty hard time with the  hand-eye coordination for that one  because the game was legit hard other  than that there were some hunting games  and  black water  oh boy  so remember how people with tin foil  hats always say the government's  watching you like they have eyes  everywhere  well microsoft came out with an  always-on living room camera with the  kinect around the time it came out that  the nsa was actually collecting data and  watching pretty much all americans like  granny on down  that royally creeped people out and they  did not want a camera with a microphone  staring at them in their living room all  the time  especially when they might be playing  games like mass effect 3 and screaming  hey shoot that guy it's interesting  because iphones were becoming popular  around the same time  and nowadays people carry around cell  phones all the time or have alexa in  their home listening to them and both of  those are far more powerful at  surveillance  [Music]  voice commands in a tactical navy seal  inspired game made a ton of sense on  paper but my roommates at school  probably thought i was not sitting in my  room screaming tactical commands at my  tv like breach bang clear socom us navy  seals came with a mostly functional mic  and headset that you could use to talk  to your ai teammates but you couldn't  just say whatever you wanted you had to  follow a specific line of commands that  your squadmates could understand and  that didn't exactly flow naturally or  work under pressure particularly in an  unforgiving milsim game the voice  commands can be really frustrating i  lost track of the number of times ai  teammates would do unexpected things  like listen to me intently and then run  over to a live grenade on the ground  like a golden retriever or throw a  flashbang right into a door frame and  back into our team it was hard to  remember how to tell your team to get  away from dangerous spots when all you  wanted to scream was get the hell away  from there even though voice commands  didn't really work well the general idea  again was ahead of its time and probably  was about as good as early  text-to-speech software think of how  many devices you talk to now i mean this  one could come back in future games it  could be really interesting to have much  more natural conversations with your ai  squad mates assuming the other people in  your real life don't mind you screaming  at televisions  [Music]  one of the hands down best inventions in  all of games is a controller rumbler a  dead simple haptic device inside of your  controller that vibrates and gives you  feedback through your hands so it makes  sense that some developers wanted to try  to apply this rumbling sensation to  other parts of your body but just  because you can doesn't mean you should  for starters a lot of them aren't taking  input from the games themselves but just  listening to sound and responding to the  audio input the feedback from getting  hit basically seems to boil down to a  locational massage and in one case a  reviewer tried a touching simulator  which got really awkward really weird  okay can you get off me alright can you  stop touching down there please can you  stop you need to learn boundaries if  you're using add-ons like head  attachments then when you play a game  you're basically getting kicked in the  head and the entire point of games  particularly shooters is to take the fun  and stimulating parts of the combat and  leave behind the traumatic parts like  pain to be fair some reviewers seem to  like the butt focus haptic feedback and  things like racing games that let you  feel the engine rumble strips and  anti-lock brakes but you're basically  just sitting on a base speaker at that  point so maybe just scoot your base  speaker on a little closer first and try  that haptic gloves on the other hand are  pretty cool i tried out the white falcon  haptic controller at ces many years ago  and i could genuinely feel the different  textures of digital objects but this  technology has not caught on to  mainstream gaming and at the same time  the humble rubble controller keeps just  getting more and more advanced see the  ps5 dual sense honestly if you play with  a haptic vest i would love to know more  and please tell me what you think below  [Music]  what wacky things you remember shooters  trying over the years that didn't work  or were just too far ahead of their time  we have a bunch more shooter focused  list videos coming soon so head on over  to gamespot's youtube page to check out  8 great sniping games you can play now  thanks for watching  [Music]  you

